Bedrock Structured Knowledge Base with S3 Tables
Redshift Serverless query engine + AWS Default Glue Data Catalog + Lake Formation
Purpose. This document captures the working setup used to connect an Amazon S3 Tables table to an Amazon Bedrock structured Knowledge Base, using Amazon Redshift Serverless as the SQL query engine. The key requirement is to expose the S3 Tables database through a resource link in the default Glue Data Catalog and grant Lake Formation permissions to the Bedrock KB service role.
1. Working architecture
S3 Tables
  fraud-poc-table-bucket
    └─ frauddetectionathena
        └─ sample_table1
              ↓
S3 Tables federated catalog
              ↓
Lake Formation resource link: finaltest
(in default Glue catalog 724772076249)
              ↓
Bedrock Structured Knowledge Base
              ↓
Redshift Serverless query engine
Important: The table does not need to become a native Redshift table. Redshift Serverless is used as the query engine; the schema/table is supplied through the AWS Default Glue Data Catalog.
2. Create the resource link in the default Glue catalog
In the AWS console, go to Lake Formation → Databases → Create resource link. Use the following values:
Field	Value
Resource link name	finaltest
Destination catalog	724772076249 (default catalog)
Shared database region	US East (N. Virginia)
Shared database	frauddetectionathena
Shared database catalog ID	724772076249:s3tablescatalog/fraud-poc-table-bucket
Validation: return to Lake Formation → Databases, choose catalog 724772076249, and verify that finaltest appears there. This is the resource link Bedrock will use through the default Glue catalog.
3. Lake Formation permissions
Principal for both grants: sandbox-bedrock-kb
3.1 Grant on the actual S3 Tables table
Go to Lake Formation → Data permissions → Grant → Named Data Catalog resources and select:
Catalog: 724772076249:s3tablescatalog/fraud-poc-table-bucket
Database: frauddetectionathena
Table: sample_table1
Permissions: SELECT, DESCRIBE
For this read-only KB use case, INSERT, DELETE, ALTER, DROP, SUPER, and grantable permissions are not required.
3.2 Grant on the resource link
Create a separate Lake Formation grant for the same role:
Catalog: 724772076249 (default catalog)
Database / resource link: finaltest
Resource link permission: DESCRIBE
Final Lake Formation permission model
sandbox-bedrock-kb
├─ Default Glue catalog: 724772076249
│   └─ finaltest
│      └─ DESCRIBE
└─ S3 Tables catalog
    └─ frauddetectionathena
       └─ sample_table1
          ├─ SELECT
          └─ DESCRIBE
4. IAM permission for the KB service role
The Bedrock Knowledge Base service role should be able to request Lake Formation data access. Ensure the role has permission equivalent to:
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "lakeformation:GetDataAccess",
  "Resource": "*"
}
5. Redshift Serverless setup
Use the same Redshift Serverless workgroup selected in the Bedrock KB configuration. For IAM authentication, create or verify an IAM database user for the KB service role and grant access to the external Glue catalog database.
CREATE USER "IAMR:sandbox-bedrock-kb" WITH PASSWORD DISABLE;

GRANT USAGE ON DATABASE awsdatacatalog
TO "IAMR:sandbox-bedrock-kb";
If the IAMR user already exists, do not recreate it.
6. Create the Bedrock structured Knowledge Base
Create a Knowledge Base with a Structured data store and configure it as follows:
Setting	Value
Query engine	Amazon Redshift
Connection option	Redshift Serverless
Workgroup	data-sbx-demo-mt1-wg
Authentication	IAM Role
KB service role	sandbox-bedrock-kb
Data schema / storage	AWS Default Glue Data Catalog
Glue catalog table	finaltest.sample_table1
Do not enter frauddetectionathena.sample_table1 in the Bedrock Default Glue Catalog field. The KB should reference the resource link path: finaltest.sample_table1
7. Sync and test
Create the KB and run Sync. A successful setup shows “Sync completed” for the data source. Then open Test and start with simple questions such as:
•	Show all transactions.
•	Which transactions are fraudulent?
•	What is the total amount of fraudulent transactions?
A successful Bedrock-generated SQL statement for this setup can look like:
SELECT *
FROM awsdatacatalog.finaltest.sample_table1;
8. Troubleshooting the main permission error
If the KB test returns an error similar to “Insufficient Lake Formation permission(s) on sample_table1,” verify these items in order:
1.	sandbox-bedrock-kb has SELECT and DESCRIBE on frauddetectionathena.sample_table1 in the S3 Tables catalog.
2.	sandbox-bedrock-kb has DESCRIBE on the finaltest resource link in the default Glue catalog.
3.	sandbox-bedrock-kb has lakeformation:GetDataAccess in IAM.
4.	The Redshift IAM database user IAMR:sandbox-bedrock-kb exists and has USAGE on awsdatacatalog.
5.	The Bedrock storage table is configured as finaltest.sample_table1.
9. Add semantic context after the base KB works
Once the plain structured KB can query the S3 Table successfully, add the semantic/generation context. Update the table reference in the YAML to the resource-link path, for example:
generation_context:
  tables:
    - name: "finaltest.sample_table1"
      description: >
        Transaction-level fraud data.
Then apply the semantic context to the KB using the same update script/API process used for the earlier structured-KB POC.
10. Deployment checklist
•	☐ S3 Table exists: frauddetectionathena.sample_table1.
•	☐ Resource link exists in default Glue catalog: finaltest.
•	☐ KB role has DESCRIBE on finaltest.
•	☐ KB role has SELECT + DESCRIBE on the actual S3 Table.
•	☐ KB role has lakeformation:GetDataAccess.
•	☐ Redshift IAMR user exists for sandbox-bedrock-kb.
•	☐ IAMR user has USAGE on awsdatacatalog.
•	☐ Bedrock KB uses Redshift Serverless as query engine.
•	☐ Bedrock storage uses AWS Default Glue Data Catalog.
•	☐ Bedrock table reference is finaltest.sample_table1.
•	☐ KB sync completes successfully.
•	☐ Plain KB test succeeds before semantic YAML is applied.
